+`make test` also runs the NET RX lane and the responder's protocol
+vectors (`make test-net-rx`, `make test-tap-responder`).
+`tools/tap_responder.py` is the host side of the TAP socket: it answers
+DHCP, ARP and ICMP echo with slirp's user-mode addressing (guest
+`10.0.2.15`, gateway `10.0.2.2`, DNS `10.0.2.3`) so a guest can actually
+get a lease and ping its gateway. `tests/nic_net.c` is a freestanding
+x86_64 payload that reproduces the guest's own protocol code and rides
+the `--kernel=` load lane; twice, byte-identical, it prints
+`[dhcp] PASS: IP 10.0.2.15, gateway 10.0.2.2` and `[net] PASS: lease +
+ARP + echo reply (payload verified)`. Run the responder by hand with:
+
+```sh
+python3 tools/tap_responder.py --timeout 30          # answers DHCP/ARP/ICMP
+python3 tools/tap_responder.py --mode=capture --out frames.hex   # record only
+python3 tools/tap_responder.py --drop=arp,icmp,dhcp  # the negative control
+```
+
+It needs `python3` and the endpoints `UDP 127.0.0.1:8080`/`:8081` free;
+`--gw-mac` overrides the gateway MAC (see ledger **D7** in
+`docs/plans/NET_PLAN.md`: that value follows slirp's published
+convention but could not be measured here).
+
 ## Architecture
 
 `src/cpu.c` is the instruction interpreter. `src/mem.c` and `src/io.c` implement the physical and port address spaces. `src/pci.c` implements PCI config mechanisms. `src/devices.c` contains the platform models and EHCI/USB storage behaviour. `src/main.c` is the CLI harness.
